Using Amazon Web Services, create and configure a relational database instance using
Amazon RDS.
Task
1. Amazon RDS from the AWS Console.
2. Create a new database using Standard Create.
3. Select MySQL or PostgreSQL.
4. Configure the DB instance class
5. Create administrator username and password.
6. Configure storage within Free Tier limits.
7. Enable public access for remote database connectivity.
8. Enable automatic backups for the database instance.
9. Connect to the database using a database client (e.g., MySQL
Workbench/psql).
10.Verify connectivity by creating a sample table and inserting records.


1. Login to AWS Console
Open
AWS Console
Login to your AWS account.
2. Open Amazon RDS

In top search bar type:

RDS
Open
Amazon Relational Database Service
3. Create Database
Left sidebar → Databases

Click:

Create database
4. Choose Database Creation Method

Under:

Choose a database creation method

Select:

Standard create
5. Choose Database Engine

Select either:

MySQL

OR

PostgreSQL

Example:

MySQL
6. Choose Version

Keep default version selected.

7. Templates

Under:

Templates

Select:

Free tier
8. Configure DB Instance
DB Instance Identifier

Enter:

mydatabase
Master Username

Enter:

admin
Master Password

Enter password.

Example:

Admin@123

Confirm password again.

9. Configure Instance Class

Under:

DB instance class

Keep Free Tier option:

db.t3.micro

OR

db.t2.micro

(depending on availability)

10. Configure Storage

Under:

Storage

Keep default Free Tier settings.

Example:

20 GiB

Storage type:

General Purpose SSD (gp2/gp3)
11. Configure Connectivity
Public Access

Under:

Connectivity

Set:

Public access → Yes
VPC Security Group

Choose:

Create new security group
12. Configure Additional Settings
Database Name

Under:

Additional configuration

Enter:

sampledb
13. Enable Automatic Backups

Under:

Backup

Ensure:

Enable automated backups

is checked.

Keep default backup retention period.

14. Create Database

Scroll down and click:

Create database
15. Wait for Database Creation

Status will show:

Creating

Wait until status changes to:

Available

(This may take 5–10 minutes)

16. Configure Security Group for Remote Access
Open EC2 Security Groups

Search:

EC2
Left sidebar → Security Groups
Open RDS Security Group
Find security group attached to RDS
Open it
Edit Inbound Rules

Click:

Edit inbound rules

Click:

Add rule
Add MySQL/PostgreSQL Rule
For MySQL

Type:

MySQL/Aurora

Port:

3306

Source:

Anywhere IPv4
OR For PostgreSQL

Type:

PostgreSQL

Port:

5432

Source:

Anywhere IPv4
Save

Click:

Save rules
17. Copy RDS Endpoint
Open Database

Go back to:

RDS → Databases
Open your database
Copy Endpoint

Under:

Connectivity & security

Copy:

Endpoint

Example:

mydatabase.abc123.ap-south-1.rds.amazonaws.com
18. Connect Using MySQL Workbench
Open MySQL Workbench

Download if needed:

MySQL Workbench

Create New Connection
Hostname

Paste RDS endpoint

Port
3306
Username
admin
Password

Enter DB password

Test Connection

Click:

Test Connection

Expected:

Successfully connected
19. Create Sample Database Table

Open SQL editor and run:

CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    age INT
);
20. Insert Records

Run:

INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Sahil',20);
INSERT INTO students VALUES (2,'Rahul',21);
21. Verify Records

Run:

SELECT * FROM students;

Expected Output:

id	name	age
1	Sahil	20
2	Rahul	21
